## 5. Custom validators, @Valid usage, validation groups

### `@Valid` usage
Applied to the `@RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto` in `OwnersApi.addOwner` (line 86), triggering Bean Validation on the DTO before the controller body runs. No validation groups are used anywhere (all `groups()` default to `{}`).

### Custom constraint `@TelephoneValidation` + `TelephoneValidator`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneValidation.java` (whole file)
```java
@Target({ ElementType.FIELD })
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = TelephoneValidator.class)
@Documented
public @interface TelephoneValidation {
    String message() default "Telephone must be convertible to E.164 form (8 to 15 digits)";
    Class<?>[] groups() default {};
    Class<? extends Payload>[] payload() default {};
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneValidator.java` (lines 12-21)
```java
public class TelephoneValidator implements ConstraintValidator<TelephoneValidation, String> {
    @Override
    public boolean isValid(String telephone, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
        if (telephone == null) {
            return true; // null is handled by the field's @NotNull constraint
        }
        return E164Telephone.isValid(telephone);
    }
}
```

The normalization/validation rules live in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/E164Telephone.java` — `normalize(raw)` (lines 30-49) strips spaces/dashes/brackets, keeps a leading `+country code`, otherwise assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0`, and requires 8-15 digits (else returns null). `isValid` is `normalize(raw) != null`.

There is also an unrelated custom validator for pets (`PetAgeValidator`/`PetAgeValidation`) that is not used by this endpoint.

### Validation error handling
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` — `@ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)` (lines 133-169) turns bean-validation failures into a 400 `ProblemDetail` with `errors` and `schemaValidationErrors`. `DataIntegrityViolationException` (line 113) maps to 404; generic `Exception` (line 96) maps to 500; `AccessDeniedException` (line 84) is rethrown (so Spring Security produces 403).

